WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.888 --> 00:00:02.648
(gentle instrumental music)

00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:04.959
- [Franklin] Every year we
lose about 20, 30 species

00:00:04.959 --> 00:00:06.400
that are gone forever,

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.790
and using this technology,
I think that we can preserve

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:14.061
and save those species
that have been lost.

00:00:14.061 --> 00:00:16.032
- [Narrator] Scientists at
the University of Georgia

00:00:16.032 --> 00:00:18.761
are attempting to use
DNA from endangered cats

00:00:18.761 --> 00:00:21.550
to save them from extinction.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:22.415
- [Franklin] What we're trying

00:00:22.415 --> 00:00:24.533
to do here is collect skin cells

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:26.870
from endangered species
and actually turn them

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.619
into sperm or eggs.

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:31.197
- [Narrator] The goal is
to develop a tiger embryo

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.870
and artificially inseminate a house cat,

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.103
which will then give
birth to a baby tiger.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.530
Because of the extensive knowledge

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.926
of domestic cats' reproductive systems,

00:00:40.926 --> 00:00:43.502
the risk of failure is significantly lower

00:00:43.502 --> 00:00:46.828
than if using a tiger as a surrogate.

00:00:46.828 --> 00:00:49.508
- We know that we're gonna
have a lot of hurdles

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:51.075
and so what we wanna do is start off

00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:53.555
with an animal we know very well,

00:00:53.555 --> 00:00:56.757
and so we thought cats
was a good starting point.

00:00:56.757 --> 00:00:58.581
(lively instrumental music)

00:00:58.581 --> 00:00:59.477
- [Narrator] The three-part process

00:00:59.477 --> 00:01:01.855
of creating an artificial embryo begins

00:01:01.855 --> 00:01:06.406
with collecting skin cell
samples from endangered species.

00:01:06.406 --> 00:01:09.333
- [Franklin] We got these
samples from Zoo Atlanta.

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:12.077
They collected the biopsies
from their Sumatran tiger

00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:14.020
and their clouded leopard.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.814
- [Narrator] Those skin
cells are then turned

00:01:15.814 --> 00:01:18.920
into stem cells, which
will later be reprogrammed

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.889
into sperm and eggs, allowing
for the creation of an embryo.

00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:25.780
- Once we have the sperm and eggs,

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.261
we'll combine them together,
we'll get a developing embryo,

00:01:29.261 --> 00:01:31.031
and then the idea is
we're gonna transfer them

00:01:31.031 --> 00:01:33.342
into a domestic cat,

00:01:33.342 --> 00:01:35.204
and hopefully in a few months,

00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:37.975
we'll have a number of
kittens from that individual.

00:01:37.975 --> 00:01:40.118
(gentle instrumental music)

00:01:40.118 --> 00:01:41.182
- [Narrator] One of
the greatest challenges

00:01:41.182 --> 00:01:43.892
that the team has faced has
been developing the technique

00:01:43.892 --> 00:01:47.006
to transform skin cells into stem cells,

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:50.911
a necessary task that they
have yet to accomplish.

00:01:50.911 --> 00:01:53.069
- [Franklin] We are still
waiting to make that leap

00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:54.876
from skin cell to stem cell,

00:01:54.876 --> 00:01:56.554
and I think we're finally on the doorstep.

00:01:56.554 --> 00:01:58.054
We're ready to go.

00:01:58.919 --> 00:02:01.245
(gentle instrumental music)

00:02:01.245 --> 00:02:03.160
- [Narrator] By collecting
and freezing skin cell samples

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.102
from various animals,
scientists are effectively

00:02:06.102 --> 00:02:08.229
providing a road to bringing back species

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:11.463
that may become extinct in the future.

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:14.222
- In a single tank, you
could have a biobank

00:02:14.222 --> 00:02:18.168
of two, 300 different species
and enough individuals

00:02:18.168 --> 00:02:20.981
in each box where you
could use this technology

00:02:20.981 --> 00:02:23.231
to repopulate lost species.

00:02:25.205 --> 00:02:26.956
I'll never give up essentially,

00:02:26.956 --> 00:02:29.101
you know, as long as I can
keep doing great science

00:02:29.101 --> 00:02:33.686
and exciting things;I just
really think endangered species

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:37.853
are important to conserve,
and they're worth the effort.

